---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Layout
  title="The Missing Night | An interactive light-pollution explainer"
  description="Turn up the city and watch the faint night disappear in this interactive explanation of skyglow."
>
  <a class="skip-link" href="#light-pollution">Skip to the sky control</a>

  <header class="site-header">
    <nav aria-label="Primary navigation">
      <a class="wordmark" href="#top" aria-label="The Missing Night home">
        <span class="wordmark-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>The Missing Night</span>
      </a>
      <div class="nav-links">
        <a href="#mechanism">Mechanism</a>
        <a href="#method">Method & sources</a>
      </div>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <main id="top">
    <section
      class="experience"
      id="sky-scene"
      data-testid="sky-scene"
      data-bortle="4"
      aria-labelledby="page-title"
    >
      <img
        class="landscape"
        src={`${base}/images/canberra-horizon.jpg`}
        alt=""
        aria-hidden="true"
      />
      <div class="sky-base" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <canvas id="star-canvas" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
      <div class="skyglow" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="ground-lift" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Interactive field note / Canberra</p>
        <h1 id="page-title">
          What did the<br />
          <em>city erase?</em>
        </h1>
        <p class="hero-intro">
          Turn up artificial light. Watch the universe lose the contrast
          contest.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="control-dock">
        <div class="control-panel">
          <div class="control-heading">
            <label for="light-pollution">Light pollution</label>
            <p id="scale-help">Bortle 1 is darkest. Bortle 9 is brightest.</p>
          </div>

          <input
            id="light-pollution"
            data-testid="sky-control"
            type="range"
            min="1"
            max="9"
            step="1"
            value="4"
            aria-describedby="scale-help"
            aria-controls="sky-scene sky-status"
          />

          <div class="scale" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="scale-tick scale-tick-key"><b>1</b><small>Dark</small></span>
            <span class="scale-tick"><b>2</b></span>
            <span class="scale-tick"><b>3</b></span>
            <span class="scale-tick"><b>4</b></span>
            <span class="scale-tick scale-tick-key"><b>5</b><small>Suburban</small></span>
            <span class="scale-tick"><b>6</b></span>
            <span class="scale-tick"><b>7</b></span>
            <span class="scale-tick"><b>8</b></span>
            <span class="scale-tick scale-tick-key"><b>9</b><small>Bright</small></span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div
          class="sky-status"
          id="sky-status"
          data-testid="sky-status"
          role="status"
          aria-live="polite"
          aria-atomic="true"
        >
          <div class="status-index">
            <span>Class</span>
            <strong id="state-level">4</strong>
            <span>/ 9</span>
          </div>
          <div class="status-copy">
            <p id="state-context">Canberra outskirts</p>
            <h2 id="state-name" data-testid="state-name">
              Rural-suburban transition
            </h2>
            <p id="state-observation" data-testid="state-observation">
              The Milky Way softens. Familiar constellations remain, while the
              quiet background stars begin to go.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mechanism" id="mechanism" aria-labelledby="mechanism-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">The mechanism</p>
        <h2 id="mechanism-title">
          The stars do not switch off.<br />The background switches on.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="mechanism-grid">
        <p class="mechanism-lead">
          Light sent upward, or reflected from the ground, scatters through the
          atmosphere. That scattered light becomes <strong>skyglow</strong>.
          It raises the brightness behind every star until the faintest signals
          no longer stand apart.
        </p>

        <ol class="light-path" aria-label="How skyglow hides faint stars">
          <li>
            <span class="light-path-index">01</span>
            <strong class="light-path-title">Misplaced light</strong>
            <p class="light-path-copy">Outdoor light escapes above the horizon.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="light-path-index">02</span>
            <strong class="light-path-title">Atmospheric scatter</strong>
            <p class="light-path-copy">
              Particles redirect some of it back toward our eyes.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="light-path-index">03</span>
            <strong class="light-path-title">Lost contrast</strong>
            <p class="light-path-copy">
              Faint stars blend into a brighter background.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="statement" aria-labelledby="statement-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">A design choice, not a fact of city life</p>
      <h2 id="statement-title">
        Natural darkness has value like clean air, water and soil.
      </h2>
      <p class="statement-copy">
        Better lighting is useful, targeted, low, controlled and warm. The loss
        above is designed, which means some of it can be designed back out.
      </p>
      <div class="statement-action">
        <a
          href="https://www.dcceew.gov.au/campaign/light-pollution"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >See Australia's six lighting principles</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="method" id="method" aria-labelledby="method-title">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Method & sources</p>
        <h2 id="method-title">A comparison, not a prediction.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="method-copy">
        <p class="method-paragraph">
          This is an <strong>illustrative simulation</strong>, not a measured
          view from a particular Canberra address. The nine positions follow
          the observational language of the Bortle Dark-Sky Scale. The changing
          star field makes its central contrast visible; it does not claim an
          exact star count.
        </p>
        <p class="method-paragraph">
          Ground image generated for this student prototype. The star field and
          skyglow are rendered in the browser so the same scene can respond to
          the control.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="source-list" aria-label="Sources">
        <li class="source-item">
          <a
            data-testid="source-link"
            href="https://skyandtelescope.org/stargazing-and-observing/how-dark-is-your-night-sky/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >Sky & Telescope / Bortle scale</a>
        </li>
        <li class="source-item">
          <a
            data-testid="source-link"
            href="https://darksky.org/resources/what-is-light-pollution/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >DarkSky / What is light pollution?</a>
        </li>
        <li class="source-item">
          <a
            data-testid="source-link"
            href="https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/biodiversity/publications/national-light-pollution-guidelines-wildlife"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >Australian Government / National guidelines</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <p class="footer-brand">The Missing Night</p>
    <p class="footer-note">Independent student work for ANU COMP4020.</p>
    <a class="footer-back" href="#top">Back to sky</a>
  </footer>

  <script src="../scripts/main.ts"></script>
</Layout>
